\DOC WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_STACK_TAC

\TYPE {WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_STACK_TAC : thm -> thm -> thm -> term -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves a Windows-ABI x86 correctness/safety specification of a wrapper function
from the corresponding System V specification of the core routine, either
closing the goal outright or leaving a single residual event-trace goal.

\DESCRIBE
{winmc : thm} is the machine-code definition of the Windows wrapper;
{stdmc : thm} is the machine-code definition of the standard (System V)
routine; {coreth : thm} is the already-proved specification of the core
routine, a universally quantified {ensures} statement, possibly wrapped in an
existential over an event-trace function; {reglist : term} is a list of the
callee-saved registers the wrapper saves and restores; {stdstackoff : int} is
the stack offset used by the standard routine. It applies to a goal {A ?- t}
with {A} empty and {t} a universally quantified implication whose conclusion is
an {ensures} statement about the Windows wrapper, optionally wrapped in an
outer existential over an event-trace function.

If the event-trace existential is absent, the goal is closed:
{
   ?- t
   ========================================  WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_STACK_TAC winmc stdmc coreth reglist stdstackoff
}
\noindent If it is present, one subgoal remains, whose conclusion {u} is an
existentially quantified equation identifying a concrete symbolic event trace
with the witness function applied to the wrapper's arguments, conjoined with a
memory-access-bounds condition:
{
   ?- t
   ========================================  WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_STACK_TAC winmc stdmc coreth reglist stdstackoff
   A' ?- u
}
\noindent Here {A'} is a large set of newly added assumptions: instantiated
non-overlapping conditions, the core routine's trace and access-bounds
properties, and facts about the final symbolic state (loaded bytes, register,
flag and stack-slot contents, the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation, and the
final event list).

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes, on goals of this single
shape.

\ENDDOC
